## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of non-trivial bit interleaving without loops, a common low-level optimization in graphics/encoding.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (Morton order)
- Mask-and-shift interleaving
- 128-bit composition from two 64-bit halves

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudocode outline (no branches)
uint128 interleaveBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  uint64_t sLo = spreadBy2(lo); // using masks 0x5555.., 0x3333.., etc.
  uint64_t sHi = spreadBy2(hi);
  r.lo = sLo;
  r.hi = sHi << 1;
  return r;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a robust, branchless 128-bit rotation, ensuring correct cross-boundary behavior and emphasis on manipulating split 64-bit halves.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across hi/lo halves
- Branchless implementation via masks and shifts
- Edge cases: n = 0, n = 64, n = 127

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
  n %= 128;
  uint128 res;
  if (n == 0) return x; // illustrative intent; prefer mask-based approach to avoid branches
  // Branchless approach would compute both paths and select via masks
  if (n < 64) {
    res.lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    res.hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
  } else {
    unsigned m = n - 64;
    res.lo = (x.hi << m) | (x.lo >> (64 - m));
    res.hi = (x.lo << m) | (x.hi >> (64 - m));
  }
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test edge cases like n=0, n=64, n=127? 
- How would you adapt this for a 256-bit type?

## Why This Is Asked
Rotations are fundamental in cryptography (e.g., SHA-2), hashing, and bitboard manipulation. Implementing them correctly on composite 128-bit types tests understanding of cross-word boundaries and careful handling of edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Decompose 128-bit value into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Handle k near 64 where rotation crosses half boundaries
- No loops required—just careful bitwise arithmetic
- Return early for k==0 to avoid undefined behavior with 64-bit shifts

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotl128(uint128 x, unsigned k) {
    k &= 127;
    if (k == 0) return x;

    if (k < 64) {
        uint64_t hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
        uint64_t lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    } else {
        unsigned k2 = k - 64;
        uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k2) | (x.hi >> (64 - k2));
        uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k2) | (x.lo >> (64 - k2));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of non-trivial bit rearrangements across 128 bits without loops. The task blends endian concepts with mask-shift patterns, common in low-level data handling at scale.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit data as hi/lo halves
- Byte-wise reversal using masks and shifts
- Endianness-aware data transformations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation outline (actual C-like code uses uint128 struct)
static uint64_t bswap64(uint64_t x){
  uint64_t t = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  t = ((t & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((t & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  return (t << 32) | (t >> 32);
}
uint128 endianSwap128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.hi = bswap64(x.lo); r.lo = bswap64(x.hi); return r;
}
```

